[[:db/retract 17592186132013 :answer-field/choices 17592186132016] [:db/retract 17592186132013 :answer-field/choices 17592186132017] [:db/retract 17592186132013 :answer-field/choices 17592186132018] [:db/retract 17592186132013 :answer-field/choices 17592186132014] [:db/retract 17592186132013 :answer-field/choices 17592186132015] {:db/id 17592186133609 :question/problem "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$, where \n\n![](images/e3/e3892b772fb778d209832e6f5e8337186a4343ff8f550c6f8457edb9ae0311e3.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/93/932bb863f1e3c1e59d7d0afc649768f193f8349c94c6e70ae1262ed5011ac34b.svg)\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by $A^T\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix $A^T \\! A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $(A^T \\! A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133608 :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/cf/cf50ed91023704d55ddb9874e9c2bd081bce5ab28fb556019d038e55f32c0905.svg)\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by $A^T\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix $A^T \\! A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $(A^T \\! A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-24 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-24 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133663 :question/problem "Consider the system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ are given below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $\\Vert A\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert.$\n![](images/3e/3e5e0da0f8e23d412833c26dc9967e443fdccb98d347eaa0481f94294093341b.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134329 :question/problem "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $Ax=b$, where \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}16 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 24\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186132013 :answer-field/correct "answer-b36fb759-7e61-5dc6-b202-80ff8e6277b6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9e0ce33d-1a7a-5210-8d79-aac72efd95ba" "answer-be2d6e42-5df1-5c0e-95a9-7be627821491" "answer-b36fb759-7e61-5dc6-b202-80ff8e6277b6" "answer-33c29b82-3f5f-5c8e-a125-2cbdd6417d8f" "answer-47817a63-5bbe-52d7-9d9c-c60e2ec34597"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9e0ce33d-1a7a-5210-8d79-aac72efd95ba" :answer/id #uuid "9e0ce33d-1a7a-5210-8d79-aac72efd95ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be2d6e42-5df1-5c0e-95a9-7be627821491" :answer/id #uuid "be2d6e42-5df1-5c0e-95a9-7be627821491" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b36fb759-7e61-5dc6-b202-80ff8e6277b6" :answer/id #uuid "b36fb759-7e61-5dc6-b202-80ff8e6277b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33c29b82-3f5f-5c8e-a125-2cbdd6417d8f" :answer/id #uuid "33c29b82-3f5f-5c8e-a125-2cbdd6417d8f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-47817a63-5bbe-52d7-9d9c-c60e2ec34597" :answer/id #uuid "47817a63-5bbe-52d7-9d9c-c60e2ec34597" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135059 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ -{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is easy to show that the system is inconsistent (i.e., it has no solution). However, in real-world problems, we might want to find an approximate solution to the system. \n\nLet's formulate the problem a little more rigorously. Firstly, we write down the system in the form $A\\mathbf x = \\mathbf b\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/22/22b8abd4638e579875755f482442cf1dbf89653bd9d396b4a802217e25177839.svg)\n\nWe are interested in vectors $\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}$ such that $A\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}$ is very close to $\\mathbf b.$ More precisely, we wish to *minimize the distance* between $A\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}$ and $\\mathbf b.$ Recall that the distance between $A\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}$ and $\\mathbf b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Vert  A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert.\n$$\n\nThe smaller the value of $\\Vert  A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert,$ the better our approximation $\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}$ will be. \n\nIn terms of vector subspaces, we wish to find a vector $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ that lies in $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ and is closest to $\\mathbf{b}.$\nTherefore, $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ must be the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{b}$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of $A.$\n\n![](images/3a/3a1c79036954f0e04212892b7e3d79c75f8cd7b81d7785441f090f89a4eb6faf.png)\n\nIt can be shown that ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ is found by solving the corresponding **normal equation**, given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe'll prove this at the end of the lesson. Note that the normal equation is obtained by left-multiplying the equation $A {\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} = \\mathbf b$ by $A^T.$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix $A^T \\! A$ is invertible. Therefore, left-multiplying the normal equation by $(A^T \\! A)^{-1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} = (A^T \\! A)^{-1}A^T \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the right-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 18\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{99}[\\begin{aligned}18 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{33} \\\\ -\\frac{38}{33}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vector ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ is called the **least-squares solution** of the given system.\n\n**Watch out!** We assumed that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, which guarantees that $A^T \\! A\\,$ is invertible. However, if the columns of $A$ are linearly *dependent,* then we have to use a different approach to solve the problem." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134026 :activity/expected-seconds 1200.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
